WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.890
Soooo 2020 has been quite the year.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:07.041
Instead of presenting a standard scare-the-
bejeezus out of you monster, let’s give

00:00:07.041 --> 00:00:09.272
our minds and our adrenaline a rest,

00:00:09.272 --> 00:00:12.070
Because isn’t that what we all need right
now?

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.630
If the demonic Nuckelavee is on the disgusting
end of the monster spectrum, the Kasogonagá

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.120
sits on the adorable opposite end.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:26.019
A sky deity from the mythology of the Toba
peoples of South America, the Kasogonagá

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:30.550
appears as a small, hairy creature with short
legs who fell out of the clouds and needs

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.989
a little human help to return to the sky.

00:00:32.989 --> 00:00:38.309
It has the ability to bring rain, lightning,
and thunder—and a history of helping the

00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:40.775
indigenous people of South America.

00:00:41.729 --> 00:00:45.049
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka and this is Monstrum

00:00:45.291 --> 00:00:51.229
The Kasogonagá hails from the Gran Chaco
region of South America, which includes modern-day

00:00:51.229 --> 00:00:53.589
Argentina, Bolivia, and Paraguay.

00:00:53.589 --> 00:00:58.670
Because it is spread across many cultures
and regions, it goes by a few names.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.989
This diminutive creature is genderless, and
its physical appearance across stories varies

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:04.699
as widely as its name.

00:01:04.699 --> 00:01:10.240
But its most common and defining characteristic
is its ability to control storms.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.440
The Kasogonagá resides in the clouds and
can be called on by a shaman, or ‘pi-oxonaq’,

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:15.440
for assistance.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.960
It can create thunder, control the rain, and
command lightning at will.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.290
Sometimes even emitting lightning from its
mouth!”

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:27.579
This furry helper is described as having a
small head and a body covered with long hair

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:33.210
like an anteater, with four tiny feet and
a long snout, or occasionally as a small,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.020
multicolored elephant.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.500
Some modern depictions of the Kasogonagá
add rainbow hair—which may just be a conflation

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:45.280
between it and another nonhuman being from
Toba mythology—the Koomonaló described

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.630
as a “rainbow snake,” that also brings
storms.

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.979
A rainbow haired anteater storm deity?

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:52.359
I love it.

00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:57.509
As with many creatures whose origins are in
oral traditions, Kasogonagá’s name, appearance,

00:01:57.509 --> 00:02:03.100
and stories are influenced by the language,
religion, and community of its various regions.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:08.479
Toba spiritual practices integrate animism,
a worldview that asserts that humans, non-human

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:14.680
animals, and nonliving entities all have spirits
or sentience, and influence the lives of people.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:19.340
Part of that belief is that most nonhuman
entities are capable of changing into whatever

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:24.720
corporeal body they wish, as if changing clothes,
meaning the altering physical depictions of

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.560
the Kasogonagá across cultures are not seen
as unusual.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:34.099
But most beliefs still center around the same
general narrative: it’s a deity who fell

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:37.099
from the sky and needs human help to return.

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:42.580
The origin story goes—a man out hunting
heard a voice in a clearing, and came upon

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:43.580
“Lightning.”

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:47.480
The creature spoke to the man, telling him
it had fallen from the sky and could not return

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.480
to the clouds.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.580
The being implored the man for help, asking
him to build a large fire so it could use

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.430
the smoke to rise back up to the heavens.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:03:01.069
The creature, too small to climb onto the
fire itself, told the man to pick it up and

00:03:01.069 --> 00:03:05.850
place it upon the fire, and after to run home
immediately.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:10.629
He follows these instructions and when Lightning
reaches the sky a violent thunderstorm covers

00:03:10.629 --> 00:03:15.850
the drought-stricken land with a heavy rain—a
sign of the entity’s gratitude.

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:21.310
Kasogonagá was so thankful it continued to
protect and watch over the man, communicating

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:24.519
with him through the air, and giving him the
power to heal people.

00:03:24.519 --> 00:03:29.579
The man became a shaman, and the grateful
sky deity promised he could call upon it at

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:34.189
any time, and if anyone bothered him, the
Kasogonagá would “strike” his enemy with

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:36.790
lightning and kill them.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.510
So this idea that a human could interact with
a nonhuman deity isn’t an odd concept for

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:42.519
the Toba people.

00:03:42.519 --> 00:03:48.019
In Animism, nonhuman entities coexist with
humans, and although not all people can see

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:51.840
or interact with them, their general existence
is agreed upon.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.549
The role of animism in everyday life of the
Toba people is further demonstrated in their

00:03:56.549 --> 00:04:02.300
retelling of historical events, which often
involves human and nonhuman intervention.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:07.440
The indigenous Toba people, also known as
the Qom, found their land infiltrated by Jesuit

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.890
missionaries in the 17th-century, and eventually
part of the Gran Chaco region was declared

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.860
national territory of Argentina in 1884.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.290
That same year, a military campaign called
the Conquest of the Chaco Desert led to violent

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:27.940
conflict with the Qom as the Argentine government
built settlements for further conquest; their

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:31.780
pursuits were physical, spiritual, and cultural.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.660
Many of the stories of this period of colonization
include the indigenous people escaping or

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:41.230
even defeating the soldiers because of communication
with entities.

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:46.060
One story tells of a Kasogonagá helping a
group of Toba women captured by the Argentine

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:50.790
military during the campaign of occupation
of indigenous communities.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.580
Six women were collecting carob fruit when
the military seized them, tied them up, and

00:04:55.580 --> 00:05:00.580
forced them to walk towards a prison camp
while the captors rode horses and donkeys.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.850
The women were bound and held captive at the
prison camp, and through communication with

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:11.030
Kasogonagá, who implored the women to escape
that night if they had any hope of survival.

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:12.100
Which they did.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.610
Because we know the story today.

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:19.680
For the Qom people, time is fluid, and history
is not always recounted exactly chronologically

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.850
and older oral traditions merge with modern
history.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:27.590
In recent years, as the Qom continue their
fight for rights to ancestral territory, non-human

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:32.780
figures, including the Kasogonagá, appear
in the retelling of these modern histories.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:37.810
According to one story, an electrical storm
allowed the Qom people to set up roadblocks

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:42.870
to prevent 100 police officers from evicting
a group of indigenous people from a local

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:43.870
school.

00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:48.130
The Qom people say that the unrelenting rain,
thunder, and lightning that afforded them

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:51.910
protection and camouflage was sent by Kasogonagá.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.310
The storm reportedly ceased only when both
roadblocks were complete.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:00.910
As a creature who still has a supportive role
to play in the lives of the Qom people today,

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:04.900
this “monster” is different from many
of the other ones I’ve covered in a few

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:05.900
ways.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:10.010
And since I frequently research monsters who
are destructive, vindictive, and downright

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:15.950
evil, I find the helpful Kasogonagá to be
a welcome relief, and a pleasant and important

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:20.153
reminder that even in 2020, not all monsters
are bad.

00:06:22.077 --> 00:06:25.309
I also would like to hug it. But I would definitely ask permission first.

00:06:25.309 --> 00:06:28.194
If only there was a plush version of it...

